WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.168
Welcome to CS104 and Logical Reasoning, Part One.

2
00:00:04.168 --> 00:00:10.420
Logic gives us a precise way to answer a deceptively simple question:

3
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:15.630
does a conclusion really follow from the information before it?

4
00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:21.882
In ordinary conversation, a claim can feel persuasive because of its topic,

5
00:00:21.882 --> 00:00:23.445
tone, or familiarity.

6
00:00:23.445 --> 00:00:29.697
Formal logic asks us to temporarily set those features aside and expose

7
00:00:29.697 --> 00:00:31.260
the underlying structure.

8
00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:37.512
We will begin with statements and logical operators, use truth tables to

9
00:00:37.512 --> 00:00:43.764
compare compound forms, study conditional statements and their related forms, and finish

10
00:00:43.764 --> 00:00:47.932
with standard rules of inference and argument validity.

11
00:00:47.932 --> 00:00:53.663
As you watch, pause before worked answers and make a prediction.

12
00:00:53.663 --> 00:00:58.352
The goal is not to memorise symbols in isolation.

13
00:00:58.352 --> 00:01:04.604
It is to develop a reliable habit: identify the form, examine the

14
00:01:04.604 --> 00:01:08.251
relevant truth conditions, and justify the conclusion.

15
00:01:08.251 --> 00:01:12.805
Our first object is a statement, sometimes called a proposition.

16
00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:18.269
A statement is a declarative sentence that is true or false, but

17
00:01:18.269 --> 00:01:19.179
not both.

18
00:01:19.179 --> 00:01:24.188
Four is an integer is a statement, and it is true.

19
00:01:24.188 --> 00:01:29.652
Paris is the capital of England is also a statement, although it

20
00:01:29.652 --> 00:01:30.563
is false.

21
00:01:30.563 --> 00:01:36.027
A command such as open the door is not a statement, because

22
00:01:36.027 --> 00:01:38.759
it does not assert a fact.

23
00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:41.946
A question is not a statement either.

24
00:01:41.946 --> 00:01:46.955
Be careful with sentences whose truth value is unknown to us.

25
00:01:46.955 --> 00:01:52.419
A statement does not stop being a statement merely because we do

26
00:01:52.419 --> 00:01:55.151
not know whether it is true.

27
00:01:55.151 --> 00:02:00.159
The requirement is that it has a truth value in principle.

28
00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:05.623
This distinction lets logic focus on assertions that can participate in truth-preserving

29
00:02:05.623 --> 00:02:06.079
reasoning.

30
00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:11.558
We represent statements using variables such as p, q, and r.

31
00:02:11.558 --> 00:02:17.536
Suppose p means Jane is a mathematics major, q means Jane is

32
00:02:17.536 --> 00:02:23.016
a computer science major, and r means Jane will take CS104.

33
00:02:23.016 --> 00:02:28.993
Replacing complete sentences with variables is not an attempt to remove meaning.

34
00:02:28.993 --> 00:02:34.473
It allows us to see the reusable pattern underneath the meaning.

35
00:02:34.473 --> 00:02:40.451
An argument about Jane, an argument about divisibility, and an argument about

36
00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:44.934
program correctness may share exactly the same logical form.

37
00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:50.912
Once a form is known to be valid, every correct substitution into

38
00:02:50.912 --> 00:02:53.901
that form inherits the same guarantee.

39
00:02:53.901 --> 00:02:59.380
Always maintain a small dictionary connecting each variable to its sentence.

40
00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:05.358
Most translation mistakes come from silently changing what a variable means or

41
00:03:05.358 --> 00:03:11.336
losing track of which expression is a premise and which is the

42
00:03:11.336 --> 00:03:11.834
conclusion.

43
00:03:11.834 --> 00:03:14.653
The simplest logical operator is negation.

44
00:03:14.653 --> 00:03:20.292
If p is a statement, not p is written with the negation

45
00:03:20.292 --> 00:03:23.581
symbol and has the opposite truth value.

46
00:03:23.581 --> 00:03:29.219
If p is Paris is the capital of England, then not p

47
00:03:29.219 --> 00:03:32.978
is Paris is not the capital of England.

48
00:03:32.978 --> 00:03:38.617
When p is true, not p is false, and when p is

49
00:03:38.617 --> 00:03:40.966
false, not p is true.

50
00:03:40.966 --> 00:03:46.604
In English, negation can appear as not, it is not the case

51
00:03:46.604 --> 00:03:49.424
that, no, never, or another phrase.

52
00:03:49.424 --> 00:03:55.062
Translate the intended meaning rather than searching mechanically for a single word.

53
00:03:55.062 --> 00:03:57.412
Also pay attention to scope.

54
00:03:57.412 --> 00:04:03.050
Negating an entire compound statement is different from negating just one component.

55
00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:08.688
Parentheses show the scope explicitly, and later De Morgan's laws will tell

56
00:04:08.688 --> 00:04:14.327
us how to move a negation through a compound expression without changing

57
00:04:14.327 --> 00:04:15.267
its meaning.

58
00:04:15.267 --> 00:04:20.236
A conjunction joins p and q with the word and.

59
00:04:20.236 --> 00:04:25.206
It is written p and q, using the wedge symbol.

60
00:04:25.206 --> 00:04:30.175
A conjunction is true only when both components are true.

61
00:04:30.175 --> 00:04:32.163
This requirement is strict.

62
00:04:32.163 --> 00:04:37.133
If either component is false, the entire conjunction is false.

63
00:04:37.133 --> 00:04:43.096
Consider Paris is the capital of England and London is the capital

64
00:04:43.096 --> 00:04:44.090
of England.

65
00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:50.053
The second component is true, but the first is false, so the

66
00:04:50.053 --> 00:04:51.544
conjunction is false.

67
00:04:51.544 --> 00:04:56.017
In a longer conjunction, every component must be true.

68
00:04:56.017 --> 00:05:01.980
This makes conjunction useful for expressing a collection of simultaneous requirements, such

69
00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:07.944
as the preconditions of an algorithm or the properties an object must

70
00:05:07.944 --> 00:05:08.441
satisfy.

71
00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:14.404
When you evaluate a compound form, compute the inner components first and

72
00:05:14.404 --> 00:05:15.895
record intermediate values.

73
00:05:15.895 --> 00:05:20.865
That simple discipline prevents many errors in longer truth tables.

74
00:05:20.865 --> 00:05:26.964
A disjunction joins p and q with or, written using the vee

75
00:05:26.964 --> 00:05:27.472
symbol.

76
00:05:27.472 --> 00:05:32.555
In mathematics, or is inclusive unless we explicitly say otherwise.

77
00:05:32.555 --> 00:05:38.654
The expression p or q is true when p is true, q

78
00:05:38.654 --> 00:05:41.703
is true, or both are true.

79
00:05:41.703 --> 00:05:46.277
It is false only when both components are false.

80
00:05:46.277 --> 00:05:52.376
Everyday language sometimes uses exclusive or, meaning exactly one of two alternatives,

81
00:05:52.376 --> 00:05:54.918
as in soup or salad.

82
00:05:54.918 --> 00:05:59.492
Do not automatically import that reading into symbolic logic.

83
00:05:59.492 --> 00:06:03.558
If exclusivity matters, it must be represented explicitly.

84
00:06:03.558 --> 00:06:06.608
Compare conjunction and disjunction as requirements.

85
00:06:06.608 --> 00:06:12.707
And asks for all listed conditions; inclusive or asks for at least

86
00:06:12.707 --> 00:06:13.215
one.

87
00:06:13.215 --> 00:06:19.314
This difference becomes especially important when we negate compound statements and when

88
00:06:19.314 --> 00:06:23.380
we translate program conditions with multiple Boolean tests.

89
00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:28.159
Compound statements can combine several operators, so order matters.

90
00:06:28.159 --> 00:06:30.814
Negation is normally performed first.

91
00:06:30.814 --> 00:06:35.592
Conjunction and disjunction are evaluated next, and implication later.

92
00:06:35.592 --> 00:06:41.964
Conjunction and disjunction have the same precedence in this course, so if

93
00:06:41.964 --> 00:06:46.743
both appear without parentheses the expression may be ambiguous.

94
00:06:46.743 --> 00:06:53.114
Parentheses are always the safest choice because they make the intended structure

95
00:06:53.114 --> 00:06:53.645
visible.

96
00:06:53.645 --> 00:07:00.017
Compare not open parenthesis p and q close parenthesis with open parenthesis

97
00:07:00.017 --> 00:07:03.202
not p close parenthesis and q.

98
00:07:03.202 --> 00:07:09.043
The first negates the entire conjunction; the second negates only p.

99
00:07:09.043 --> 00:07:14.353
These forms do not generally have the same truth value.

100
00:07:14.353 --> 00:07:20.724
Think of a statement form as a syntax tree: evaluate the deepest

101
00:07:20.724 --> 00:07:26.034
grouped operations, then work outward one layer at a time.

102
00:07:26.034 --> 00:07:32.146
A statement form is an expression built from statement variables and logical

103
00:07:32.146 --> 00:07:32.655
operators.

104
00:07:32.655 --> 00:07:38.766
It becomes an actual statement when concrete statements are substituted for the

105
00:07:38.766 --> 00:07:39.276
variables.

106
00:07:39.276 --> 00:07:45.387
A truth table examines the form systematically by listing every possible assignment

107
00:07:45.387 --> 00:07:46.915
of truth values.

108
00:07:46.915 --> 00:07:50.480
With one variable there are two rows.

109
00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:54.045
With two variables there are four rows.

110
00:07:54.045 --> 00:07:57.610
With three variables there are eight rows.

111
00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:03.212
In general, n variables require two to the power n rows.

112
00:08:03.212 --> 00:08:09.324
Arrange the rows in a consistent pattern so no assignment is duplicated

113
00:08:09.324 --> 00:08:10.343
or omitted.

114
00:08:10.343 --> 00:08:14.926
Then create a column for each useful intermediate expression.

115
00:08:14.926 --> 00:08:21.038
The table is not busywork: it is a complete proof about the

116
00:08:21.038 --> 00:08:27.149
truth behaviour of the form because it checks every possible substitution pattern.

117
00:08:27.149 --> 00:08:32.326
Here the expression is not p and q, and p or q.

118
00:08:32.326 --> 00:08:34.483
Work from the inner columns.

119
00:08:34.483 --> 00:08:36.640
First compute p and q.

120
00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:37.935
Negate that result.

121
00:08:37.935 --> 00:08:40.092
Separately compute p or q.

122
00:08:40.092 --> 00:08:43.112
Finally combine those two columns with and.

123
00:08:43.112 --> 00:08:48.289
The expression is true exactly when at least one of p or

124
00:08:48.289 --> 00:08:50.877
q is true, but not both.

125
00:08:50.877 --> 00:08:53.034
That is the exclusive-or pattern.

126
00:08:53.034 --> 00:08:58.211
Notice how an operation we did not treat as primitive can be

127
00:08:58.211 --> 00:09:00.800
constructed from negation, conjunction, and disjunction.

128
00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:05.977
This is a common theme in logic and computing: a small set

129
00:09:05.977 --> 00:09:08.997
of operators can express many useful conditions.

130
00:09:08.997 --> 00:09:14.174
At this point, pause and predict the result when p is true

131
00:09:14.174 --> 00:09:15.899
and q is false.

132
00:09:15.899 --> 00:09:21.076
The conjunction p and q is false, its negation is true, and

133
00:09:21.076 --> 00:09:25.822
p or q is true, so the final expression is true.

134
00:09:25.822 --> 00:09:31.844
Two statement forms are logically equivalent when their truth values match for

135
00:09:31.844 --> 00:09:34.855
every possible assignment of their variables.

136
00:09:34.855 --> 00:09:38.869
We write an equivalence symbol between the forms.

137
00:09:38.869 --> 00:09:44.891
Logical equivalence is stronger than two sentences happening to share a truth

138
00:09:44.891 --> 00:09:45.895
value today.

139
00:09:45.895 --> 00:09:50.913
It says the forms can never disagree under any substitution.

140
00:09:50.913 --> 00:09:56.935
A truth table proves equivalence when the final columns are identical row

141
00:09:56.935 --> 00:09:57.939
by row.

142
00:09:57.939 --> 00:10:03.961
For example, p or q and q or p always match, so

143
00:10:03.961 --> 00:10:05.466
disjunction is commutative.

144
00:10:05.466 --> 00:10:11.488
Double negation, not not p, also has the same truth value as

145
00:10:11.488 --> 00:10:13.495
p on every row.

146
00:10:13.495 --> 00:10:19.517
Equivalent forms may be substituted for one another during a derivation without

147
00:10:19.517 --> 00:10:23.532
changing the truth conditions of the larger expression.

148
00:10:23.532 --> 00:10:29.052
This is why equivalence laws function like algebraic identities for logic.

149
00:10:29.052 --> 00:10:34.190
De Morgan's laws describe how negation interacts with and and or.

150
00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:39.796
Not open parenthesis p and q close parenthesis is equivalent to not

151
00:10:39.796 --> 00:10:41.664
p or not q.

152
00:10:41.664 --> 00:10:47.270
Not open parenthesis p or q close parenthesis is equivalent to not

153
00:10:47.270 --> 00:10:49.138
p and not q.

154
00:10:49.138 --> 00:10:54.276
To understand the first law, ask what makes a conjunction fail.

155
00:10:54.276 --> 00:10:59.882
It fails when at least one component is false, so its negation

156
00:10:59.882 --> 00:11:02.684
says not p or not q.

157
00:11:02.684 --> 00:11:07.356
To understand the second, ask what makes a disjunction fail.

158
00:11:07.356 --> 00:11:12.961
Inclusive or is false only when both components are false, so its

159
00:11:12.961 --> 00:11:16.231
negation says not p and not q.

160
00:11:16.231 --> 00:11:21.836
When a negation moves through parentheses, negate every component and switch the

161
00:11:21.836 --> 00:11:22.303
operator.

162
00:11:22.303 --> 00:11:27.909
Remembering both actions prevents the common error of changing only the variables

163
00:11:27.909 --> 00:11:30.711
while leaving the connecting operator unchanged.

164
00:11:30.711 --> 00:11:35.330
A small library of equivalence laws makes simplification efficient.

165
00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:41.489
Commutative laws allow the order of p and q to switch under

166
00:11:41.489 --> 00:11:43.029
and or or.

167
00:11:43.029 --> 00:11:47.648
Associative laws allow regrouping repeated conjunctions or repeated disjunctions.

168
00:11:47.648 --> 00:11:53.807
Distributive laws expand one operator across the other, much like multiplication distributes

169
00:11:53.807 --> 00:11:59.453
over addition, although in logic both operators distribute over each other.

170
00:11:59.453 --> 00:12:05.611
Identity laws say p and true is p, while p or false

171
00:12:05.611 --> 00:12:06.638
is p.

172
00:12:06.638 --> 00:12:12.797
Domination laws say p or true is true, while p and false

173
00:12:12.797 --> 00:12:13.823
is false.

174
00:12:13.823 --> 00:12:17.416
These laws are not rules of inference.

175
00:12:17.416 --> 00:12:23.575
An equivalence replaces one expression with another expression having exactly the same

176
00:12:23.575 --> 00:12:24.601
truth values.

177
00:12:24.601 --> 00:12:30.760
Keep that distinction clear when you later work with premises and conclusions.

178
00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:36.544
Other useful laws include idempotence, double negation, De Morgan's laws, and absorption.

179
00:12:36.544 --> 00:12:42.328
Idempotence says repeating the same condition changes nothing: p and p is

180
00:12:42.328 --> 00:12:45.702
p, and p or p is p.

181
00:12:45.702 --> 00:12:51.486
Absorption says p or open parenthesis p and q close parenthesis reduces

182
00:12:51.486 --> 00:12:57.270
to p, because when p is true the whole disjunction is already

183
00:12:57.270 --> 00:13:02.572
true, and when p is false the conjunction cannot rescue it.

184
00:13:02.572 --> 00:13:08.356
Its dual form is p and open parenthesis p or q close

185
00:13:08.356 --> 00:13:10.284
parenthesis equivalent to p.

186
00:13:10.284 --> 00:13:14.140
Do not apply laws only by visual resemblance.

187
00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:19.925
Name the subexpression that plays each role, perform one justified replacement, and

188
00:13:19.925 --> 00:13:20.889
preserve parentheses.

189
00:13:20.889 --> 00:13:26.673
A clear sequence of small equivalence steps is easier to verify than

190
00:13:26.673 --> 00:13:28.601
a large unexplained jump.

191
00:13:28.601 --> 00:13:34.631
To show that two forms are not logically equivalent, one counterexample is

192
00:13:34.631 --> 00:13:35.133
enough.

193
00:13:35.133 --> 00:13:41.163
Consider not open parenthesis p and q close parenthesis and not p

194
00:13:41.163 --> 00:13:42.670
and not q.

195
00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:46.690
Let p be true and q be false.

196
00:13:46.690 --> 00:13:52.720
The conjunction p and q is false, so its negation is true.

197
00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:58.750
But not p is false and not q is true, making their

198
00:13:58.750 --> 00:13:59.755
conjunction false.

199
00:13:59.755 --> 00:14:04.278
The two forms disagree, so they cannot be equivalent.

200
00:14:04.278 --> 00:14:10.308
A complete truth table will display every disagreement, but for non-equivalence the

201
00:14:10.308 --> 00:14:12.820
first counterexample settles the question.

202
00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:18.850
This suggests an efficient strategy: understand what each form requires and deliberately

203
00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:21.865
choose values likely to separate them.

204
00:14:21.865 --> 00:14:27.895
Counterexamples are a powerful theme throughout discrete mathematics; they refute universal claims

205
00:14:27.895 --> 00:14:32.417
by showing one permitted case where the claim fails.

206
00:14:32.417 --> 00:14:35.166
We now turn to conditional statements.

207
00:14:35.166 --> 00:14:40.662
A conditional has form if p, then q, written p implies q.

208
00:14:40.662 --> 00:14:45.701
The statement p is the hypothesis and q is the conclusion.

209
00:14:45.701 --> 00:14:49.823
The conditional does not assert that p is true.

210
00:14:49.823 --> 00:14:55.320
It says that in every case where p is true, q must

211
00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:56.694
also be true.

212
00:14:56.694 --> 00:15:00.817
Think of p as triggering a promise about q.

213
00:15:00.817 --> 00:15:06.314
If you do not hand in homework, then you will get zero

214
00:15:06.314 --> 00:15:06.772
marks.

215
00:15:06.772 --> 00:15:12.268
If the hypothesis occurs and the conclusion does not, the promise is

216
00:15:12.268 --> 00:15:12.726
broken.

217
00:15:12.726 --> 00:15:18.223
If the hypothesis does not occur, that particular promise has not been

218
00:15:18.223 --> 00:15:18.681
violated.

219
00:15:18.681 --> 00:15:24.178
This reading explains the truth table and helps separate logical implication from

220
00:15:24.178 --> 00:15:24.636
causation.

221
00:15:24.636 --> 00:15:30.132
A conditional can be logically true even when the two component statements

222
00:15:30.132 --> 00:15:31.965
have no causal connection.

223
00:15:31.965 --> 00:15:37.668
The truth table for p implies q has exactly one false row:

224
00:15:37.668 --> 00:15:40.995
p is true and q is false.

225
00:15:40.995 --> 00:15:44.797
When both are true, the promise is fulfilled.

226
00:15:44.797 --> 00:15:50.501
When p is false, the conditional is true regardless of q; this

227
00:15:50.501 --> 00:15:52.877
is sometimes called vacuous truth.

228
00:15:52.877 --> 00:15:58.581
It may initially feel unusual, so focus on what the conditional forbids.

229
00:15:58.581 --> 00:16:04.284
It forbids a true hypothesis with a false conclusion and says nothing

230
00:16:04.284 --> 00:16:04.760
further.

231
00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:10.463
Suppose the rule is if a shape is a square, then it

232
00:16:10.463 --> 00:16:11.889
has four sides.

233
00:16:11.889 --> 00:16:17.592
A triangle does not violate the rule, whether or not some other

234
00:16:17.592 --> 00:16:23.296
property is true of it, because the triangle does not satisfy the

235
00:16:23.296 --> 00:16:23.771
hypothesis.

236
00:16:23.771 --> 00:16:29.475
In proofs, this truth condition allows us to establish a conditional by

237
00:16:29.475 --> 00:16:35.178
showing the hypothesis cannot occur, although such arguments should still be explained

238
00:16:35.178 --> 00:16:35.653
clearly.

239
00:16:35.653 --> 00:16:39.891
A conditional is logically equivalent to not p or q.

240
00:16:39.891 --> 00:16:44.976
Compare their truth tables: both are false only when p is true

241
00:16:44.976 --> 00:16:46.671
and q is false.

242
00:16:46.671 --> 00:16:49.638
This equivalence helps us rewrite everyday sentences.

243
00:16:49.638 --> 00:16:54.723
You get to work on time or you are fired has form

244
00:16:54.723 --> 00:16:55.994
p or q.

245
00:16:55.994 --> 00:17:01.079
To express it as an if-then statement, view p or q as

246
00:17:01.079 --> 00:17:03.198
not not p or q.

247
00:17:03.198 --> 00:17:08.283
It becomes: if you do not get to work on time, then

248
00:17:08.283 --> 00:17:09.555
you are fired.

249
00:17:09.555 --> 00:17:12.521
The same equivalence gives the correct negation.

250
00:17:12.521 --> 00:17:16.759
Negating p implies q means negating not p or q.

251
00:17:16.759 --> 00:17:20.149
De Morgan's law produces p and not q.

252
00:17:20.149 --> 00:17:25.234
In words, the negation of if p then q is p occurs

253
00:17:25.234 --> 00:17:26.929
and q does not.

254
00:17:26.929 --> 00:17:32.014
Merely reversing or negating the conditional arrow does not produce its logical

255
00:17:32.014 --> 00:17:32.438
negation.

256
00:17:32.438 --> 00:17:37.632
When implications appear inside larger forms, operator precedence becomes important.

257
00:17:37.632 --> 00:17:39.710
Negation is evaluated first.

258
00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:42.307
Conjunction and disjunction come next.

259
00:17:42.307 --> 00:17:46.981
Implication is performed last unless parentheses override the order.

260
00:17:46.981 --> 00:17:53.214
For example, p or not q implies r should be read as

261
00:17:53.214 --> 00:17:58.408
open parenthesis p or not q close parenthesis implies r.

262
00:17:58.408 --> 00:18:04.641
In formal work, add parentheses even when the conventional order would technically

263
00:18:04.641 --> 00:18:06.199
resolve the expression.

264
00:18:06.199 --> 00:18:10.354
Precision helps both the writer and the reader.

265
00:18:10.354 --> 00:18:16.587
When building a truth table, create columns in evaluation order: not q,

266
00:18:16.587 --> 00:18:20.742
then p or not q, then the conditional.

267
00:18:20.742 --> 00:18:26.975
This mirrors the syntax of the form and makes it easier to

268
00:18:26.975 --> 00:18:28.533
locate an error.

269
00:18:28.533 --> 00:18:34.766
An unexplained final column is difficult to audit; well-chosen intermediate columns turn

270
00:18:34.766 --> 00:18:38.921
the computation into a visible chain of reasoning.

271
00:18:38.921 --> 00:18:43.805
Every conditional p implies q has three commonly discussed relatives.

272
00:18:43.805 --> 00:18:47.711
The contrapositive is not q implies not p.

273
00:18:47.711 --> 00:18:50.641
The converse is q implies p.

274
00:18:50.641 --> 00:18:54.548
The inverse is not p implies not q.

275
00:18:54.548 --> 00:18:58.943
A conditional is always logically equivalent to its contrapositive.

276
00:18:58.943 --> 00:19:04.803
The converse and inverse are equivalent to each other, but neither is

277
00:19:04.803 --> 00:19:07.245
generally equivalent to the original.

278
00:19:07.245 --> 00:19:13.105
Suppose if Howard can swim across the lake, then Howard can swim

279
00:19:13.105 --> 00:19:14.570
to the island.

280
00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:20.430
Its contrapositive says if Howard cannot swim to the island, then he

281
00:19:20.430 --> 00:19:22.872
cannot swim across the lake.

282
00:19:22.872 --> 00:19:28.732
That follows: anyone capable of the longer swim is capable of the

283
00:19:28.732 --> 00:19:29.708
shorter one.

284
00:19:29.708 --> 00:19:35.568
The converse claims that swimming to the island guarantees swimming across the

285
00:19:35.568 --> 00:19:38.010
lake, which may be false.

286
00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:41.917
Reversing the arrow changes what is being guaranteed.

287
00:19:41.917 --> 00:19:45.493
The phrase only if often causes translation errors.

288
00:19:45.493 --> 00:19:49.068
P only if q means p implies q.

289
00:19:49.068 --> 00:19:54.432
It says p cannot happen without q, so q is necessary for

290
00:19:54.432 --> 00:19:54.879
p.

291
00:19:54.879 --> 00:20:00.243
A useful test is to paraphrase: if p occurs, then q must

292
00:20:00.243 --> 00:20:00.690
occur.

293
00:20:00.690 --> 00:20:04.713
By contrast, p if q means q implies p.

294
00:20:04.713 --> 00:20:07.842
Here q is enough to guarantee p.

295
00:20:07.842 --> 00:20:13.206
Pay attention to the word order instead of assuming the first clause

296
00:20:13.206 --> 00:20:14.994
is always the hypothesis.

297
00:20:14.994 --> 00:20:20.358
For example, you may enter only if you have a ticket means

298
00:20:20.358 --> 00:20:23.933
if you enter, then you have a ticket.

299
00:20:23.933 --> 00:20:28.850
It does not say that everyone with a ticket necessarily enters.

300
00:20:28.850 --> 00:20:33.767
The ticket is necessary under this sentence, but not declared sufficient.

301
00:20:33.767 --> 00:20:39.131
Writing a temporary variable dictionary and drawing the arrow explicitly is the

302
00:20:39.131 --> 00:20:40.472
safest translation method.

303
00:20:40.472 --> 00:20:46.430
A biconditional p if and only if q is written p if-and-only-if

304
00:20:46.430 --> 00:20:46.927
q.

305
00:20:46.927 --> 00:20:52.885
It is true when p and q have the same truth value

306
00:20:52.885 --> 00:20:55.864
and false when their values differ.

307
00:20:55.864 --> 00:21:01.822
The biconditional is equivalent to the conjunction of both directions: p implies

308
00:21:01.822 --> 00:21:04.305
q and q implies p.

309
00:21:04.305 --> 00:21:09.270
Therefore, proving an if-and-only-if statement normally requires two separate arguments.

310
00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:12.249
First assume p and establish q.

311
00:21:12.249 --> 00:21:15.229
Then assume q and establish p.

312
00:21:15.229 --> 00:21:21.187
The abbreviation iff, spelled i f f, is common in mathematical writing.

313
00:21:21.187 --> 00:21:24.662
Do not treat it as decorative emphasis.

314
00:21:24.662 --> 00:21:27.642
It states both necessity and sufficiency.

315
00:21:27.642 --> 00:21:33.600
A program is correct if and only if it produces correct answers

316
00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:39.558
for every allowed input means correctness guarantees the output condition, and the

317
00:21:39.558 --> 00:21:44.523
output condition also guarantees correctness under the definition being used.

318
00:21:44.523 --> 00:21:50.100
Necessary and sufficient conditions describe the two sides of an implication.

319
00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:56.183
In p implies q, p is sufficient for q because p guarantees

320
00:21:56.183 --> 00:21:56.690
q.

321
00:21:56.690 --> 00:22:02.773
The statement q is necessary for p because p cannot occur without

322
00:22:02.773 --> 00:22:03.280
q.

323
00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:09.363
For the voting example, if John is eligible to vote then John

324
00:22:09.363 --> 00:22:11.391
is at least eighteen.

325
00:22:11.391 --> 00:22:17.474
Eligibility is sufficient for the age conclusion, while being at least eighteen

326
00:22:17.474 --> 00:22:19.502
is necessary for eligibility.

327
00:22:19.502 --> 00:22:25.585
The sentence does not say that age alone is sufficient; other eligibility

328
00:22:25.585 --> 00:22:27.106
requirements may exist.

329
00:22:27.106 --> 00:22:33.189
A condition is both necessary and sufficient exactly when the biconditional holds.

330
00:22:33.189 --> 00:22:39.272
When reading a theorem, label the hypothesis as a sufficient condition for

331
00:22:39.272 --> 00:22:45.356
the conclusion and the conclusion as a necessary condition for the hypothesis.

332
00:22:45.356 --> 00:22:50.932
This gives you a reliable way to unpack unfamiliar mathematical language.

333
00:22:50.932 --> 00:22:56.450
An argument is a sequence of statements ending with a conclusion.

334
00:22:56.450 --> 00:22:58.959
The earlier statements are premises.

335
00:22:58.959 --> 00:23:04.979
An argument form is valid when no matter which concrete statements replace

336
00:23:04.979 --> 00:23:10.999
its variables, if all premises are true, the conclusion is also true.

337
00:23:10.999 --> 00:23:13.507
Validity is a structural guarantee.

338
00:23:13.507 --> 00:23:19.527
It does not say that the premises in a particular argument are

339
00:23:19.527 --> 00:23:20.530
actually true.

340
00:23:20.530 --> 00:23:26.550
Consider: if Zeus is human, then Zeus is mortal; Zeus is not

341
00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:29.560
mortal; therefore Zeus is not human.

342
00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:35.079
This uses a valid form even though the topic is mythical.

343
00:23:35.079 --> 00:23:41.099
Conversely, an invalid argument may happen to end with a true conclusion.

344
00:23:41.099 --> 00:23:47.119
We evaluate validity by asking whether true premises and a false conclusion

345
00:23:47.119 --> 00:23:52.135
are possible together—not by asking whether the conclusion sounds believable.

346
00:23:52.135 --> 00:23:57.868
A rule of inference is an argument form already known to be

347
00:23:57.868 --> 00:23:58.346
valid.

348
00:23:58.346 --> 00:24:04.079
Direct implication, often called modus ponens, has form p implies q, p,

349
00:24:04.079 --> 00:24:05.034
therefore q.

350
00:24:05.034 --> 00:24:10.767
If three divides the sum of the digits of two hundred fifty-eight,

351
00:24:10.767 --> 00:24:13.634
then three divides two hundred fifty-eight.

352
00:24:13.634 --> 00:24:18.889
Three does divide the digit sum, so three divides the number.

353
00:24:18.889 --> 00:24:24.622
Contrapositive implication, or modus tollens, has form p implies q, not q,

354
00:24:24.622 --> 00:24:26.055
therefore not p.

355
00:24:26.055 --> 00:24:31.788
If three divided the digit sum of one hundred ten, it would

356
00:24:31.788 --> 00:24:33.221
divide the number.

357
00:24:33.221 --> 00:24:38.954
Three does not divide one hundred ten, so it does not divide

358
00:24:38.954 --> 00:24:40.387
the digit sum.

359
00:24:40.387 --> 00:24:46.120
These rules move from premises to a conclusion; they are different from

360
00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:51.853
equivalence laws, which replace one expression with another identical in truth value.

361
00:24:51.853 --> 00:24:57.456
Specialisation allows us to infer either component from a conjunction: p and

362
00:24:57.456 --> 00:24:58.857
q, therefore p.

363
00:24:58.857 --> 00:25:04.459
If n divides six and n divides fifteen, then in particular n

364
00:25:04.459 --> 00:25:05.393
divides six.

365
00:25:05.393 --> 00:25:10.996
Elimination, also called disjunctive syllogism, combines p or q with not p

366
00:25:10.996 --> 00:25:12.397
to conclude q.

367
00:25:12.397 --> 00:25:17.999
If n is even or m is odd, and n is not

368
00:25:17.999 --> 00:25:20.334
even, then m is odd.

369
00:25:20.334 --> 00:25:24.069
Notice the exact role of the negated premise.

370
00:25:24.069 --> 00:25:29.672
From p or q and p, we may not conclude not q

371
00:25:29.672 --> 00:25:34.341
because the disjunction is inclusive; both components could be true.

372
00:25:34.341 --> 00:25:39.943
Matching a situation to a rule requires matching every premise, including negations

373
00:25:39.943 --> 00:25:41.344
and connective types.

374
00:25:41.344 --> 00:25:46.947
State the rule and show the substitution whenever the match might not

375
00:25:46.947 --> 00:25:47.881
be obvious.

376
00:25:47.881 --> 00:25:49.339
Transitivity chains conditionals.

377
00:25:49.339 --> 00:25:55.170
From p implies q and q implies r, conclude p implies r.

378
00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:59.058
If eighteen divides n, then nine divides n.

379
00:25:59.058 --> 00:26:02.946
If nine divides n, then three divides n.

380
00:26:02.946 --> 00:26:06.834
Therefore, if eighteen divides n, three divides n.

381
00:26:06.834 --> 00:26:09.750
Division into cases handles a disjunction.

382
00:26:09.750 --> 00:26:15.581
From p or q, p implies r, and q implies r, conclude

383
00:26:15.581 --> 00:26:16.067
r.

384
00:26:16.067 --> 00:26:21.899
If n is positive or n is negative, and either case implies

385
00:26:21.899 --> 00:26:25.301
n is nonzero, then n is nonzero.

386
00:26:25.301 --> 00:26:31.133
The cases must cover the possibilities given by the disjunction, and each

387
00:26:31.133 --> 00:26:34.048
case must reach the same conclusion.

388
00:26:34.048 --> 00:26:37.936
These patterns appear constantly in proofs and algorithms.

389
00:26:37.936 --> 00:26:43.768
Recognising them frees attention for the mathematical content while preserving a checkable

390
00:26:43.768 --> 00:26:44.740
logical skeleton.

391
00:26:44.740 --> 00:26:49.359
A truth table provides a general test for validity.

392
00:26:49.359 --> 00:26:53.977
Build columns for every premise and for the conclusion.

393
00:26:53.977 --> 00:27:00.136
Then identify the critical rows, the rows where all premises are true

394
00:27:00.136 --> 00:27:00.649
simultaneously.

395
00:27:00.649 --> 00:27:04.754
Only those rows matter to the validity guarantee.

396
00:27:04.754 --> 00:27:10.913
If the conclusion is true in every critical row, the argument form

397
00:27:10.913 --> 00:27:11.939
is valid.

398
00:27:11.939 --> 00:27:18.097
If even one critical row has a false conclusion, that row is

399
00:27:18.097 --> 00:27:21.690
a counterexample and the form is invalid.

400
00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:27.848
Rows with a false premise cannot violate the definition because validity promises

401
00:27:27.848 --> 00:27:31.440
what happens when all premises are true.

402
00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:37.598
This method also explains why an argument with inconsistent premises is technically

403
00:27:37.598 --> 00:27:40.678
valid: there are no critical rows.

404
00:27:40.678 --> 00:27:46.836
In ordinary mathematical writing, however, we prefer informative arguments with premises that

405
00:27:46.836 --> 00:27:48.375
can be satisfied.

406
00:27:48.375 --> 00:27:51.751
Let us finish with a logic puzzle.

407
00:27:51.751 --> 00:27:57.537
Exactly one of four suspects killed Ama, and exactly one suspect is

408
00:27:57.537 --> 00:27:58.983
telling the truth.

409
00:27:58.983 --> 00:28:02.359
Suspect one says suspect three killed Ama.

410
00:28:02.359 --> 00:28:06.698
Suspect two says suspect four did not kill Ama.

411
00:28:06.698 --> 00:28:12.002
Suspect three says suspects one and four did not kill Ama.

412
00:28:12.002 --> 00:28:16.342
Suspect four says suspect three did not kill Ama.

413
00:28:16.342 --> 00:28:20.199
Represent these statements, then test each possible killer.

414
00:28:20.199 --> 00:28:25.021
If suspect one is the killer, several statements are true.

415
00:28:25.021 --> 00:28:29.843
The same problem occurs for suspect two and suspect three.

416
00:28:29.843 --> 00:28:35.629
If suspect four is the killer, suspect one's claim is false, suspect

417
00:28:35.629 --> 00:28:41.415
two's claim is false, and suspect three's conjunction is false because suspect

418
00:28:41.415 --> 00:28:43.344
four did kill Ama.

419
00:28:43.344 --> 00:28:48.166
Suspect four's statement is true because suspect three did not.

420
00:28:48.166 --> 00:28:53.952
Exactly one statement is true, so suspect four is the unique solution.

421
00:28:53.952 --> 00:28:58.446
We have built a toolkit for precise reasoning.

422
00:28:58.446 --> 00:29:05.187
Statements carry truth values and combine through negation, conjunction, disjunction, implication, and

423
00:29:05.187 --> 00:29:05.748
biconditional.

424
00:29:05.748 --> 00:29:10.804
Truth tables examine every assignment and establish logical equivalence.

425
00:29:10.804 --> 00:29:17.545
De Morgan's laws and other identities let us rewrite forms without changing

426
00:29:17.545 --> 00:29:18.107
meaning.

427
00:29:18.107 --> 00:29:24.847
A conditional is false only when its hypothesis is true and its

428
00:29:24.847 --> 00:29:31.588
conclusion false; it is equivalent to not p or q, and its

429
00:29:31.588 --> 00:29:34.959
contrapositive is equivalent to the original.

430
00:29:34.959 --> 00:29:40.576
Necessary and sufficient conditions describe the direction of the implication.

431
00:29:40.576 --> 00:29:47.317
Finally, rules of inference give valid moves from premises to a conclusion,

432
00:29:47.317 --> 00:29:52.372
while critical truth-table rows provide a general validity test.

433
00:29:52.372 --> 00:29:55.181
Now complete the mastery check.

434
00:29:55.181 --> 00:30:01.922
When feedback reveals an error, return to the relevant section, explain the

435
00:30:01.922 --> 00:30:05.854
rule in your own words, and retry.
